u['questions'][0]['a']='Letrozole is usually taken every day without a planned treatment-free week. Some people have a different prescribed frequency because of liver problems. Follow your own prescription and check before changing it.'
u['questions'].append({'q':'Will before-surgery treatment delay my operation automatically?','a':'No. Your team agrees when to assess the response and when surgery should take place. The NCCP pathway describes a course of about four to eight months to allow tumour reduction; inadequate response requires a discussion of surgery or another treatment. New symptoms or changes should be reported sooner.'})
u['endocrine_timeline']['notes']=[u['administration_text'],'A before-surgery response review, an adjuvant duration review and an advanced-disease scan answer different questions; use the milestones for your setting.']

u=updates['00376']
u['indication_text']='Exemestane is used for selected postmenopausal hormone-sensitive breast cancer after surgery, as extended endocrine treatment or for advanced disease after earlier treatment. The team checks which previous endocrine medicine you received and why a switch or extension is appropriate. If switching after tamoxifen, the earlier years count towards the intended total treatment plan.'
u['administration_text']='Swallow the tablet whole, preferably after a meal; do not chew or crush it. Skip a missed dose and continue at the next scheduled time; do not double the dose.'
u['agents'][0]['explanation']='Exemestane switches off aromatase, the enzyme that makes oestrogen in tissues after menopause. This reduces the hormone signal that can encourage hormone-sensitive breast cancer cells to grow. The body makes new enzyme over time, so tablets must still follow the prescribed daily schedule. It does not reliably switch off functioning ovaries; any ovarian suppression must be prescribed and checked separately.'
u['consent_support']='The kidney and liver advice for exemestane differs from some other aromatase inhibitors: a routine dose reduction is not specified, but organ problems still need clinical review. Tell the pharmacist about all medicines and supplements because some can make exemestane less effective. Do not increase the dose yourself. Confirm menopause and any separate ovarian-suppression plan.'
for q in u['questions']:
    if q['q']=='How long will treatment continue?':q['a']='If you switch after tamoxifen, the earlier years count towards the intended total endocrine course. Switching does not automatically mean starting a new full course. The team confirms your intended total and whether a later extension is appropriate. Advanced-disease treatment continues according to benefit and tolerance.'
u['questions'].append({'q':'Can another medicine make exemestane less effective?','a':'Yes. Some medicines for seizures or infections, and St John’s wort, can reduce its effect. Check every new prescription, over-the-counter medicine and supplement with the oncology pharmacist. Do not compensate by taking extra exemestane.'})
u['endocrine_timeline']['notes']=[u['administration_text'],'Count earlier endocrine treatment when reviewing an adjuvant switch. Extended treatment is a separate decision.']

facts={
'00254':{'mechanism':'Nonsteroidal, reversible aromatase inhibitor; reduces peripheral conversion of androgens to oestrogens. It is not an ovarian-suppression drug or an ER degrader.','administration':'1 mg PO daily, continuous; swallow whole with/without food; missed or vomited dose not replaced.','duration':'NCCP consultant-directed duration according to setting, benefit and tolerance. Distinguish initial adjuvant, switch after 2–3 years tamoxifen, and advanced treatment. Do not universalise indefinite treatment or a five-year reset.','organ_function':'NCCP no renal adjustment including haemodialysis; severe renal impairment requires caution. No mild/moderate hepatic reduction; linked SmPC cautions moderate/severe hepatic dysfunction. NCCP severe hepatic disease not recommended: withhold pending review rather than invent a reduction.','source_scope':'NCCP includes adjuvant and switch indications; linked Anastrozole Teva SmPC has narrower advanced indication wording. NCCP eligibility also mentions tamoxifen contraindication/intolerance.'},
'00371':{'mechanism':'Nonsteroidal, reversible aromatase inhibitor; oestrogen deprivation rather than ER antagonism or ovarian suppression.','administration':'2.5 mg PO daily, continuous, with/without food; missed dose take when remembered unless next due within about 2–3 h, then skip; no doubling.','duration':'NCCP neoadjuvant 4–8 months to allow tumour reduction with response/surgery review; inadequate response prompts surgery and/or other options. Adjuvant, extended and advanced duration are separate.','organ_function':'NCCP no renal adjustment including haemodialysis expected; HPRA CrCl <10 lacks sufficient data and requires careful benefit/risk review. Child–Pugh A/B no adjustment. C: NCCP 50% original average dose = 2.5 mg every other day; HPRA close supervision without this numerical instruction. Reconcile current reduced/custom schedule first.','source_scope':'NCCP 00371d broad HR-positive neoadjuvant wording; SmPC additionally HER2-negative, chemotherapy unsuitable and immediate surgery not indicated. Separate optional fields flag known conflicts; missing values remain unassessed.'},
'00376':{'mechanism':'Steroidal, irreversible aromatase inactivator. It mimics an androgen substrate and inactivates the enzyme; it does not degrade the oestrogen receptor. This mechanism does not establish superiority or interchangeable sequencing with nonsteroidal AIs.','administration':'25 mg PO once daily, continuous, preferably after a meal; swallow whole without chewing/crushing; skip missed dose.','duration':'NCCP broad adjuvant/extended/advanced pathways. Aromasin label specifically 2–3 years tamoxifen then exemestane to total 5 years; extension is separate. Advanced continuation follows benefit/tolerance.','organ_function':'NCCP no renal/hepatic dose adjustment, haemodialysis expected no adjustment; linked SmPC caution. Strong CYP3A4 inducer reduces exposure: review, no Irish-source automatic 50 mg dose.','source_scope':'NCCP broader than the labelled switch regimen. Current HPRA Aromasin PDF is 9 January 2025 despite NCCP reference date 28 August 2025.'}}
common={'menopause':'Confirmed postmenopausal endocrine state. Uncertain menopause or age <55 after hysterectomy requires hormone confirmation (FSH/LH/oestradiol). Amenorrhoea after chemotherapy is insufficient proof; return of ovarian function needs review. OFS combinations require a separately prescribed regimen.','monitoring':'NCCP baseline FBC, renal and liver profile and biochemical menopause checks when indicated. Bone density and lipids are risk/clinically directed, without an invented chemotherapy-cycle cadence, ANC/platelet ladder or routine ECG/echo. Exemestane additionally consider baseline 25-OH-vitamin D per SmPC.','bone_guidance':'NICE baseline adjuvant AI DXA except already on adjuvant bisphosphonates; 2025 joint guidance risk-based BMD reassessment 1–2 yearly for lower-risk patients, around 2 yearly on bone-protective treatment. These are guidance-based intervals, not NCCP routine cycle tests or AI hold thresholds.','support':'No routine steroid/antiemetic/G-CSF for these monotherapies. NCCP advises daily calcium/vitamin D supplementation and weight-bearing exercise; select the supplement product and dose with attention to diet, deficiency, organ function and existing supplements, without inventing a universal dose. Review adherence, joints, vasomotor symptoms, sexual/vaginal symptoms and interactions early.','cross_suite':'Patient guide, knowledge, consent, support, passport and timeline should share setting-specific duration, independent OFS boundary, symptom escalation and source-aware monitoring. Keep clinical trial numbers in clinician evidence only.'}
write('service-facts.json',{'release':'0.84.20','checked_date':DATE,'regimens':facts,'common_clinician_facts':common,'patient_updates':updates,'baseline_content_findings':['All three patient evidence objects retained numerical trial outcomes in prose despite metrics=[]; replace entire evidence objects, not only metrics.','Exemestane patient indication and duration incorrectly attributed narrower product-label tamoxifen-switch scope to NCCP; corrected distinction.','Letrozole patient and KB described neoadjuvant label restrictions but assessment did not check entered conflicts.','Letrozole did not protect an already reduced/custom schedule from an automatic Child–Pugh C frequency output.','Letrozole patient urgency omitted the NCCP/SmPC tendon warning.','All three KB drug mechanisms reused an identical AI paragraph; distinguish reversible nonsteroidal inhibition from irreversible steroidal inactivation in clinician content.','KB source limitations still claimed no downloaded PDF or visual table inspection; now obsolete and must be replaced.','BIG 1-98 KB headline stopped at the 8.7-year report; add the 12.6-year follow-up with non-significant DFS estimate and observational-extension limitations.'],'integration_notes':['patient_updates values replace the named top-level keys, preserving unrelated anatomy, source and chapter content.','Use evidence.json exclusively in clinician evidence/KB; do not insert numerical trial outcomes into patient content.','Replace stale v0.84.2 KB material for these protocol_ids and update source provenance; do not duplicate profiles.','Sources attached to baseline surveillance rows were retained; the new audit specifically rechecked NCCP, HPRA, NICE DXA and AIBL2025.']})
